ushes you in a steady linear  sweep north as you slowly reclaim  territory and asks you to regularly  bring resources back to your base in  that starting area to bolster it so  what's to stop you from just darting  ahead  well damage numbers new dawn introduces  RPG elements like damage numbers into  its design for the first time in this  series the game's guns and enemies fall  into four different tiered ranks and  getting your head requires you to go out  in the world to scavenge crafting  materials to upgrade your base so you  can upgrade your weapons workshop and  eventually craft better guns to take  down higher rank enemies impeding your  progress outfits armor and defense  numbers don't factor into your growth  just weapons guns at rank one and two  will do a minimal amount of damage to  well armoured rank three in elite  ranking meets early on this can be  annoying if you try to push the limits  of the game in a way you're not meant to  heading too far into the map and needing  to use up hundreds of bullets to take  down our rank three bear you encounter  isn't as terrifying as much as it is  silly and eventually the demands of the  story missions will stop you from going  too far  but if you dial down your Far Cry 5  style expectations of freedom and go  with the flow you'd run into these  awkward predicaments far less often your  guns feel like they do the damage  they're supposed to and enemies feel  like they have an acceptable level of  resistance in fact once you get access  to the top tier arsenal things will  start to swing wildly in your favor your  guns will feel overpowered to the point  where even shooting Rank 1 enemies in  the foot might be enough to take them  out which feels great when you're  getting overwhelmed if you play it the  right way the game's IBG style systems  basically feel invisible and you can  enjoy Far Cry style of weighty gunplay  and feel like an incredibly competent  one-person army the feeling of  eventually being able to overcome new  Dawn's elite enemies is good but you're  eventually left wondering why you needed  to be held back by artificial gaining at  all it doesn't help that there's no  tangible sense of growth with weapons  and vehicle crafting new dawn selections  of guns and  isn't dramatically different enough  between ranks to make the large  distinction and damage output believable  rank one weapons are a varied suite of  handguns rifles and shotguns and high  ranked Arsenal's are basically defined  by the increasing amount of duct tape  and junk on that same suite as if that  stuff had magical properties then made  the guns perform better there are a lot  of guns to choose from but if he's  played Far Cry 5 you'll immediately  recognize them duct tape or no the one  nice exception is the noose or launcher  which fires circular saw blades higher  two versions of the weapon actually have  noticeably different properties like the  ability to shoot saw blades with  ricocheting homing and boomerang  properties it's the only weapon which  truly feels like it was born out of the  post apocalypse improvised from  scavenged parts aerosol cans pipes and  spray-paint may give the other guns and  cars a cool look but they don't change  how familiar they feel the same can be  said of the world itself Far Cry 5s Hope  County already felt a little bit  post-apocalyptic the rural setting was  isolated from the world thanks to  antagonist Joseph C so even though there  are plenty of visible differences to the  region the impact of those changes isn't  massive there are a few key locations  that provoke some amusement in their  discovery but the strength of Far Cry  soap County was its natural environments  the forests the lakes the rivers and  mountains the conceit that the region  was revegetated by a super blue muffin  nuclear devastation means that the vibe  in New Dawn is basically identical  despite dramatic increases in upended  cars and graffiti it's a pretty post  apocalypse but it doesn't have the  feeling of desperation you might  associate with the theme scavenging for  materials doesn't feel like a drastic  necessity it's just a way to get ahead  new dawn doesn't feel like it takes the  theme to enough of an extreme to make it  feel meaningful or different the solid  bones of farc rise combat and  progression are still here though and  they're still very good taking on  outposts within your rank whether that  be by stealth or aggression is still  enjoyable and the game encourages you to  repeat them at increased difficulties to  earn more resources  new dawn also introduces self-contained  emissions called expeditions these are  large diverse maps set outside herp  County and they feature set pieces like  a New Orleans amusement park an aircraft  carrier and even a Splinter Cell themed  plane crash expedition environments are  a highlight but the snatch-and-grab  objectives mean that you're never really  encouraged to stop and appreciate them  you're more concerned with getting the  hell out of there as a non-stop stream  of enemies comes after you the concise  nature of the game means there's a  remarkable lack of time given to  appreciate the characters and plot - a  few of the major characters feel like  they could be interesting the twin  sister antagonists especially but the  few interactions you have with them are  definitely not enough to develop them  and to make you care while the  performances have gusto key moments of  pathos just feel completely unearned  undeveloped connections to characters  also exacerbate the relative mundanity  of the story missions compared to the  games side and open wall activities and  talking tired sequences bland chases  forced melee fights and even a slow boat  ride all of which just go on for way too  long  you do get a double jump though that's  right a double jump as in the ability to  jump in midair you also get the ability  to basically turn invisible and give  yourself super speed and strength  now the Far Cry series has always  dabbled in the mystic but yes in a  strange turn of events you don't  eventually says [ __ ] it and gives you  access to superhuman powers and the way  it changes how you approach the world is  undoubtedly the best thing about the  game these sudden powers let you lean  hard into superhero fantasy allowing you  to do things like bound of offenses and  onto buildings using your newfound  mobility and invisibility to completely  terrorize enemies like you're the  predator or perhaps you're jumping high  into the fray and firing off explosive  arrows pretending you're Hawkeye from  the avengers maybe you're more of a  Wolverine activating the Berserker  ability to rush an outpost at SuperSpeed  and send heavily armed assailants and  bears are like flying with just your  bare fists the powers are so good that  it's almost a shame that they come at a  point late in the game where you'll  likely already be well equipped to deal  with elite ranked enemies since a few  scenarios that challenge your ability to  use these effectively definitely would  have been a welcome addition  but as  stands that our fantastic expansion of  fire cries combat vocabulary they  completely elevate your confidence to  rip through everything and everyone  suddenly turning new Dawn's familiar  pedestrian experience into a raucous  blast there's a lot of potential and the  ideas seeded in you dawn but there isn't  enough room for many of them to breathe  so none of them feel like they realize  their full potential not the post  apocalypse themes not the RPG mechanics  not the weapons vehicles plot or  characters  regardless progressing through the  adventure is an enjoyable experience  especially once you get your superhuman  powers but this is largely because Far  Cry 5s combat and progression models  remain compelling enough to propel you  forward for its part though you don't as  a palatable but unremarkable spin-off  that feels like it could have ach